{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgdZDuGnJtjHBWgA4eJAX","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The ADT deal comes from a buyer with a particular structure. Thrive Holdings is a holding company spun out of Josh Kushner's venture firm Thrive Capital, and its stated mission is to hold companies long-term and revitalize them with AI. It says it holds over 75 companies. The article added the holding company description in an update, which suggests the distinction from the venture parent matters for how the purchase should be read.\n\nThe price is $10 per share, about $7.6 billion in equity. ADT puts the total enterprise value at $15.3 billion. The difference is roughly $7.7 billion, which in general reflects obligations such as debt that come with the business. The article does not break the figure down, so what that amount consists of is not established here.\n\nTaken together, the stated intent of long-term ownership and the size of the transaction mean ADT is positioned as an anchor. The press release says ADT \"will form the foundation of Thrive Holdings' smart home security business.\" Thrive and ADT said they plan to invest and release new products but did not say which ones, and the article reports no results from Thrive's AI approach at ADT or at other portfolio companies.\n\nOpenAI is both an investor in Thrive Holdings and a partner, supplying models, technology and forward-deployed engineers to its companies. How much of that support goes to ADT is not stated. The first evidence of what this acquisition means will be the products the two companies eventually announce, in a market where Apple and Amazon have recently launched AI-related home security offerings of their own."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":903,"cache_read_input_tokens":3809,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ep